\begin{abstract} 
Hypergraphs offer a powerful framework for modeling higher-order interactions that traditional pairwise graphs cannot fully capture. However, practical constraints often lead to their simplification into projected graphs, resulting in substantial information loss and ambiguity in representing higher-order relationships. In this work, we propose \method, a supervised approach for reconstructing the original hypergraph from its projected graph by leveraging edge multiplicity.
%that incorporates both theoretical guarantees and multiplicity-aware classifier. 
To overcome the difficulties posed by the large search space,
\method integrates several key ideas: (a) identifying provable size-$2$ hyperedges, which reduces the candidate search space, (b) predicting the likelihood of candidates being hyperedges by utilizing both structural and multiplicity-related features, and (c) not only targeting promising hyperedge candidates but also examining less confident ones to explore alternative possibilities.
Together, these ideas enable \method to efficiently and effectively explore the search space.
In our experiments using 10 real-world datasets, \method achieves up to 74.51\% higher reconstruction accuracy compared to state-of-the-art methods.

% Hypergraphs offer a powerful framework for modeling higher-order interactions that traditional pairwise graphs cannot fully capture. However, practical constraints often lead to their simplification into projected graphs, resulting in substantial information loss and ambiguity in representing higher-order relationships. Addressing these challenges, we introduce \method, a supervised method for hypergraph reconstruction that incorporates both theoretical guarantees and multiplicity-aware classifier. \method leverages theoretical guarantees to identify provable hyperedges, reducing the candidate search space, while its classifier predicts the likelihood of candidate hyperedges by utilizing both structural and multiplicity-related features. \method explores both the most and least probable hyperedge candidates, ensuring high-confidence candidates are selected while diversifying the search space by breaking down uncertain candidates to prevent redundancy in subsequent iterations. This strategy enhances reconstruction accuracy by effectively leveraging edge multiplicity and reducing search space complexity. Experimental results on 10 real-world datasets demonstrate \method’s ability to achieve up to 74.51\% higher reconstruction accuracy compared to state-of-the-art methods.

\end{abstract}

