\subsection{Swath-Distilling: Coordinated Masking and Wavelet Enhancement}
% To learn the comprehensive information possessed by multimodal data, we integrate multimodal data and train a UNet network for scanning swath precipitation retrieval. The method that integrates multimodal data for training outperforms any single-modality training approach, and the overall performance surpasses GPM 2B-CMB (one of the SPPs). Details about this UNet are provided in the supplementary material. To facilitate the generalization of precipitation retrieval from multimodal data to single-modality (i.e., IR), we utilize knowledge distillation and designate this UNet as our teacher model.
We first integrate multimodal data (IR, PMW, and PR) and train a teacher UNet model for scanning swath precipitation retrieval. This multimodal fusion significantly outperforms single-modality baselines and performs better than GPM 2B-CMB\cite{TheGPMCombinedAlgorithm}, a leading satellite precipitation product (SPP) (see Supplementary \ref{teacher_details} for architecture and ablation studies).  
To generalize this capability from swath-scale to full-disc precipitation retrieval, we employ feature-based knowledge distillation, where a student PRE-Net model, restricted to IR-only inputs, aligns its feature maps with the teacher’s multimodal representations.%especially using feature-based KD to align the feature maps of a PRE-Net restricted to IR-only inputs with the pre-trained teacher model.

However, the teacher’s multimodal inputs and the student’s IR-only inputs create a significant feature representation gap.
Conventional feature-based KD struggles to bridge this asymmetry, as the student lacks access to the teacher’s complementary PMW/PR data.
%In the PRE task, the teacher model receives multimodal data, while the student model relies solely on IR data, resulting in a significant disparity in feature representation between the two. 
%As a result, vanilla feature-based KD struggles to effectively align feature maps, causing the student model to fail in replicating the teacher's stronger representation ability. 
