To enhance feature learning during KD, we leverage Masked Image Modeling (MIM). MIM masks random input patches and trains models to reconstruct missing features from visible context.
%, thereby learning robust spatial correlations. 
A well-known advancement in MIM is Masked Autoencoder (MAE)~\cite{mae}.
%, which leverages an asymmetric encoder-decoder architecture to efficiently reconstruct heavily masked inputs.
While MAE demonstrates exceptional performance at high mask ratios (e.g., 75\% masking), its integration in the Masked Knowledge Distillation (MKD)~\cite{mkd} reveals a critical mismatch: MKD achieves optimal distillation effectiveness at low mask ratios (~ 20\% masking), failing to exploit MAE’s high-masking reconstruction strengths. 
This mismatch between MAE’s high-masking design and MKD’s low-masking operational regime creates inefficiencies, as MKD fails to fully exploit MAE’s reconstruction capabilities under its intended high-masking paradigm. This inefficiency limits the student’s ability to recover high-quality precipitation patterns from partial multimodal observations (i.e., IR only).

%To enhance feature learning during KD, Masked Image Modeling (MIM) is a promising way. MIM masks random patches of input data and trains models to reconstruct missing information from visible context, thus the model can learn the feature correlation among context.
%A prominent advancement in MIM is the Masked Autoencoder (MAE)~\cite{mae}, which leverages an asymmetric encoder-decoder architecture to efficiently reconstruct heavily masked inputs. While MAE demonstrates exceptional performance at high mask ratios (e.g., 75\% masking), its integration into Masked Knowledge Distillation (MKD)~\cite{mkd} reveals a critical contradiction: MKD achieves optimal distillation effectiveness at low mask ratios (e.g., 20\% masking). This mismatch between MAE’s high-masking design and MKD’s low-masking operational regime creates inefficiencies, as MKD fails to fully exploit MAE’s reconstruction capabilities under its intended high-masking paradigm.

%However, the contradiction that MKD performs optimally at lower mask ratios while MAE excels at higher mask ratios leads to inefficiency in MKD. 
To resolve this contradiction, we propose the Coordinated Masking and Wavelet Enhancement (CoMWE) module, which adaptively balances high-ratio masking with detail preservation. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig2}, CoMWE combines two components: RMKD and DAWE. The process of CoMWE can be expressed as follows:
%we and effectively encode richer information with a high mask ratio that preserves details, we develop the Coordinated Masking and Wavelet Enhancement (CoMWE) module, comprising RMKD and DAWE submodules, as illustrated in Fig.\ref{fig:fig2}. The process of CoMWE can be expressed as follows:
% \cite{mgd,mkd,maeeff,DMKD}.

% Vanilla KD struggles to capture the intricate relationships between multimodal data and precipitation because students merely imitate the teacher's representations instead of xxx thus failed to xxx\cite{DMKD}. Introducing Masked Image Modeling (MIM) into KD is an effective solution \cite{mgd,mkd,maeeff,DMKD}. The core concept involves concealing random patches of students' features and compelling students to restore corresponding missing features through visible patches. This approach enhances student's learning capability by understanding potential relationships between masked and visible regions. To comprehensively encode rich information effectively, we developed the Coordinated Masking and Wavelet Enhancement (CoMWE) module, comprising RMKD and DAWE submodules, as illustrated in Fig.\ref{fig:fig2}. The process of CoMWE can be expressed as follows:

\begin{equation}
    F_i^c=MaskedConv(\phi(F_{i-1}^c)\times M_i^c;\theta_{MC}),
    \label{eq:eq1}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    F^h_i = DAWE(embed(X_{IR})),
    \label{eq:eq2}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    F_i^R=AE((F_i^c + F^h_i)\times M_i^R;\theta_{AE}),
    \label{eq:eq3}
\end{equation}
where $MaskedConv$ and $AE$ represent multi-scale masked convolutions and auto-encoders. $\theta_{MC}$ and $\theta_{AE}$ are the corresponding weights. $\phi$ is a max-pooling and convolutional layer with kernel size 1 × 1 to align the down-sampling channel dimension. $F_i^c$, $F^h_i$ and $F_i^R$ represent multi-scale convolution features, high-frequency prompt features, and reconstructed features, respectively. $M_i^c, M_i^R$ are masks for multi-scale masked convolutions and auto-encoders. Eq.\ref{eq:eq2} will be described in the following part.

