\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:problem_def}
Modern scientific and engineering applications - from partial differential equation (PDE) discretizations ~\cite{elliptic-near-linear} to sparsification ~\cite{spielman2008graph}, spectral graph partitioning~\cite{spectral-partition} and graph-based learning \cite{JMLR:learn-lap, learn-graph} - routinely generate large, sparse linear systems whose solution critically depends on efficiently handling Laplacian matrices. In this paper, we are interested in developing a high performance algorithm for solving such sparse linear system:
\begin{equation*}
    L \, x = b,
\end{equation*}
where $L$ is a Laplacian matrix (a.k.a., graph Laplacian), which we will define later. %Many scientific problems are not inherently Laplacian, but if they are symmetric diagonally dominant M-matrix or simply SDD, they can be converted to Laplacian linear systems~\cite{nonnegative, combinatorial-sdd}.
Our approach generalizes to situations where $L$ is symmetric diagonally dominant (SDD)~\cite{nonnegative, combinatorial-sdd}. 

% \emph{Fill-ins} are the nonzero entries that emerge in a sparse matrix during the factorization process—entries that were originally zero in the input matrix. For example, in Gaussian elimination or Cholesky factorization, eliminating a variable often introduces new connections among the remaining variables, resulting in additional nonzeros. In our paper, fill-ins also refer to new connections between nodes that are already connected (i.e. multi-graph). These fill-ins can significantly affect both the computational complexity and memory usage of the factorization, as they alter the sparsity structure of the matrix from what was initially provided.\david{should define what fill-in means. factored lower diagonal matrix can be much denser than original matrix. this leads to both space and runtime overheads. the space is a problem on GPU because GPU RAM is small. the runtime is a problem because we want to be fast.}

The two approaches for solving sparse Laplacian systems are direct methods and iterative methods. Direct methods such as Cholesky factorization can leverage high-performance BLAS-3 operations~\cite{lapack99, demmel1999asynchronous} but often struggle because of the computational and memory overhead incurred by fill-ins during factorization~\cite{Davis2016DirectSurvey, cohen2018solving}. \emph{Fill-ins} are the nonzero entries that emerge in a sparse matrix during the factorization process—entries that were originally zero in the input matrix. Unfortunately, direct methods typically require a significant amount of computation and storage, which are prohibitive for large problem sizes.


% As an example of traditional direct methods, classical Cholesky factorization   is often plagued by uncontrolled fill-ins during elimination, which significantly increases computational overhead and impedes parallel scalability \cite{kyng2016approximate}. 
On the other hand, iterative solvers typically require much less computation and memory per iteration but may struggle to converge for ill-conditioned linear systems without a high-quality 
preconditioner~\cite{saad-iterative}. Pre-conditioners have been extensively studied by both high-performance computing (HPC) and theoretical computer science (TCS) communities~\cite{jambulapati2021ultrasparse,vishnoi2013lx}. One heavily researched preconditioner is the incomplete Cholesky (ichol) method, which is known for its simplicity, ease of use and parallel construction~\cite{incomplete-chol, chow2015fine, anzt2018parilut, kim2016task}. Another approach is the algebraic multigrid method (AMG)~\cite{STUBEN2001281}, which is highly efficient and typically outperforms the other approaches for linear systems arising from the discretization of PDEs. To address graph Laplacians, researchers have designed specialized AMG methods~\cite{livne2012lean, amg-for-laplacian}.
%Although highly efficient and typically outclass the other approaches, AMG-based algorithms are often difficult to use and configure.

In recent years, there has been significant interest in sampling based randomized preconditioners~\cite{kyng2016approximate, gao2023robust,murray2023randomized}. One can draw similarities between these randomized preconditioners and ichol since they both seek to maintain sparsity by dropping fill-ins during Gaussian elimination. However, the difference is that sampling based 
preconditioners
%rather than dropping entries based on non-zero locations or values,
%\xslnote{ichol can also do threshold dopping; suggeest removing "rather .."}
focus on preserving statistical properties, such as approximating the exact factor in expectation while maintaining sparsity. As an example, the algorithm may choose to scale an entry based on the probability that it gets sampled. In particular, the randomized 
preconditioner by Gao, Kyng, and Spielman has shown promise as it outperforms ichol on a variety of problems~\cite{gao2023robust}. However, from a HPC perspective, the parallelism-compatible  construction of such preconditioners is under-explored. There have been some follow-up works that explore the parallelization of such preconditioners. Sachdeva and Zhao proposed a theoretical framework for parallel block sampling method using random walk~\cite{sachdeva2023simple}. Rchol, a CPU shared memory parallel implementation, requires long preprocessing time since it computes a recursive nested-dissection~\cite{nested-dissection} to decompose graph into independent domains before parallel elimination~\cite{rchol}. Recently, Baumann and Kyng also developed a theoretical framework for parallelizing Laplacian linear equation solvers \cite{baumann2024}.

%\section{Main Contribution}
In this paper, we propose a new parallelization method ParAC for the approximate Cholesky (AC) randomized incomplete algorithm~\cite{gao2023robust}. ParAC dynamically identifies parallelism during execution despite random fill-in positions. Furthermore, unlike many previous incomplete factorization methods, ParAC does not require running a costly nested-dissection to set up the parallel pipeline~\cite{rchol, kim2016task}. We implement ParAC for both CPU and GPU with different strategies. 
On CPU, ParAC improves upon previous methods because it no longer requires a nested-dissection ordering. On GPU, we employ a persistent kernel approach, which, when combined with our parallel strategy, can also be extended to other standard sparse factorization routines. We benchmark our implementation against several state-of-the-art methods for solving sparse Laplacian systems, including HyPre~\cite{hypre}, AmgX~\cite{naumov2015amgx} and %\xslnote{Matlab's} 
ichol, and discuss some of the intuitions that enabled our algorithm to be competitive. 
% \david{can we include 1 or 2 stats about how we outperform the comparison systems? e.g., our cpu implementation was over 10x faster than SOTA on most tested datasets} 


Experimental results show that ParAC achieves phenomenal results on GPU with simple strategies such as random permutation and sorting-based elimination ordering, thereby almost completely eliminates the heavy pre-processing for the symbolic factorization stage of an incomplete factorization type of preconditioners. This is especially useful if there are not many right-hand side vectors $b$ in the linear systems, or if we are dealing with situations where the input changes every round, such as incremental sparsification. It is worth noting that ParAC, combined with sketching~\cite{murray2023randomized}, provides a fast framework for graph sparsification~\cite{spielman2008graph, fast-multiply, resparsification}. 

