\section{Cholesky Factorization for Laplacian}
To provide an in-depth analysis, we introduce the graph  theoretic framework to describe the factorization procedure of Laplacian matrix as graph transformations~\cite{rosetarjan78}. 
\begin{definition}[Graph Laplacian] \label{d:gl}
We consider a weighted undirected graph $\G=(V,E)$, with the vertex set $V=(v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_N)$, edge set $E=\{ e_{ij}:v_i,v_j\in V \}$ and an edge $e_{ij} = (v_i,v_j) \in E$ carries weight $w_{ij}>0$. The graph Laplacian of $\G$ is defined as 
\begin{equation} \label{e:l}
L = [\ell_{ki}]_{k,i=1}^{N} = \sum_{e_{ij} \in E} w_{ij} \, \bb_{ij} \bb_{ij}^\top,
\end{equation}
where $\bb_{ij}=\be_i-\be_j$, the difference of two standard bases $\be_i,\be_j \in \mathbb{R}^N$ (the order of difference between $\be_i,\be_j$ does not affect $L$).
\end{definition}
This construction means that every edge in the underlying weighted, undirected graph $\G$ contributes an outer-product term whose structure inherently captures the difference between connected vertices. %[CITATION NEEDED FROM OTHER PAPER.]

