\subsection{Classical Cholesky}
\label{sec:classic}
%Classical Cholesky factorization is distinct from the general background provided in Section~\ref{sec:problem_def}. Given a Laplacian matrix $\mathbf{L} \in \mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$, the goal is to compute matrices $\mathbf{G}$ (lower triangular) and $\mathbf{D}$ (diagonal) so that $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{G}^T$ approximates $\mathbf{L}$. Algorithm~1 accomplishes this via iterative elimination of vertices and corresponding Schur complement updates.

Classical Cholesky factorization for a Laplacian matrix can be interpreted
as a sequence of operations on the graph $\G$ associated with the Laplacian
matrix $L$~\cite{sachdeva2023simple, kyng2016approximate}. Consider the Cholesky decomposition on the Laplacian matrix
$L$ during the $k$th step, the algorithm extracts the $k$th column
of $L$, normalizing it by $\sqrt{\ell_{kk}}$. This step corresponds
to isolating vertex $k$'s contribution and its incident edges. The elimination of vertex $k$ involves updating $L$ via the Schur-complement:
\begin{equation}
    L=L-\frac{1}{\ell_{kk}}L(:,k)L(k,:). \label{a:standard-chol-step}
\end{equation}
Graphically, this operation removes vertex $k$ from the graph. However,
rather than discarding the connectivity information, the elimination
induces new edges among the neighbors of $k$, effectively constructing
a clique. The new edge connecting two neighbors $i$ and $j$ is assigned
a weight given by $\frac{\ell_{ki}\ell_{kj}}{\ell_{kk}}$, preserving
the overall influence of the eliminated vertex.

This operation is known as a \emph{contraction} of the graph $\mathcal{G}$, where vertex $k$ is removed and its
neighbors become fully connected.
%interconnected. %Contraction is central to the graphical interpretation.
Each elimination step maintains the form of a Laplacian since the
updated matrix continues to be expressed as a sum of Laplacian matrices
corresponding to the remaining graph and the newly formed clique.
The procedure continuously updates the matrix until every vertex has
been processed. 

