However, such an approach produces dense fill-in patterns that often lead to significant computational and memory overhead. In large-scale sparse Laplacian matrices, this full interconnection amplifies both runtime and storage requirements---a phenomenon explored in \cite{kyng2016approximate,davis2006direct,george1981computer, liu1990role}. In parallel processing settings, particularly on many-core architectures, the accumulation of these fill-ins becomes a bottleneck in both memory and communication costs, limiting scalability \cite{davis2006direct,liu1990role}. These considerations motivate the development of alternative strategies, such as randomized factorization methods.

