Before we explain how sampling works, we first define the graph Laplacian of the sub-graph consisting of $k$ and its neighbors as
% \david{why are we defining the graph laplacian stuff here? isn't this interpretation true for classic cholesky too?}
\begin{equation}
L^{(k)} \triangleq \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}_k} \, (-\ell_{ki}) \,\, \bb_{ki} \bb_{ki}^\top
\end{equation}

%~\cite{kyng2016approximate},
% Therefore, one can write the elimination at \cref{a:rchol:eliminate} in 
% \cref{a:rchol} as the sum of two Laplacian matrices
Therefore, one can write the elimination described by \cref{a:standard-chol-step} as the sum of two Laplacian matrices, which is also a Laplacian:
\begin{equation}
   L - \frac{1}{\ell_{kk}} \, L(:, k) \, L(k, :) 
=
\underbrace{L - L^{(k)}}_{\text{Laplacian matrix}} +
\underbrace{L^{(k)} - \frac{1}{\ell_{kk}}L(:, k)L(k, :)}_{\text{Laplacian matrix}}
\label{equation:sum_of_lap} 
\end{equation}


% When vertex $v_k$  is eliminated, its neighbors (the set  $\mathcal{N}_k$ defined by nonzero entries in the  $k-\mathrm{th}$ row/column) receive updates that create new interactions among them. Specifically, the sampling or deterministic update introduces a clique structure (as seen in Equation \eqref{eq:clique}) among the neighbors. %Choosing vertex$v_k$ at the kth step ensures that the updates are localized—only the subgraph corresponding to the neighbors $\mathcal{N}_k$ are affected. This preserves a level of sparsity that is vital for both computational efficiency and for designing parallel strategies (especially as more vertices are processed).
% \david{This text is in the randomized section but it seems to be about the classical deterministic algorithm because we are adding the full clique. why isn't this in section 2?}

The first term is the graph Laplacian of the sub-graph consisting of all edges except the ones connected to $k$. Since  
\[
L(:,k)  - L^{(k)}(:,k) = 0, \quad L(k,:) - L^{(k)}(k,:) = 0,\label{eq:clique}
\]
we know $L - L^{(k)}$ zeros out the $k$-$\mathrm{th}$ row/column in $L$ and updates the diagonal entries in $L$ corresponding to $\mathcal{N}_k$.

The second term
\begin{equation} \label{e:clique}
    L^{(k)} - \frac{1}{\ell_{kk}}L(:, k)L(k, :) = 
    \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j \in \mathcal{N}_k} \frac{\ell_{ki} \, \ell_{kj}}{\ell_{kk}} \, \bb_{ij} \bb_{ij}^\top
\end{equation}
is the graph Laplacian of the clique among neighbors of $k$, where the edge between neighbor $i$ and neighbor $j$ carries weight ${\ell_{ki} \, \ell_{kj}}/{\ell_{kk}}$.
%\david{Note that there is not yet any prose description of Algorithm 2 (randomized cholesky) or its sample clique subroutine.}

Now we begin the discussion on AC. In essence, AC tries to preserve the entry-wise expectation of \cref{e:clique} using sampling methods. In contrast to the full clique updates used in the classical scheme, algorithm~\ref{a:rchol} (AC algorithm)\cite{kyng2016approximate, gao2023robust} introduces randomization to selectively sample fill-ins during vertex elimination, addressing the pitfalls of dense fill-in and high memory requirements described above. As with the classical method, AC iterates over vertices $k$ from $1$ to $N-1$. For each vertex, if the corresponding column of $L$ is non-empty, the algorithm normalizes that column by dividing by $\ell_{kk}$, recording the result in $G$ and updating the diagonal accordingly.

The key difference lies in how AC updates $L$ during elimination. Rather than forming a complete clique among all neighbors in $\mathcal N_k$, Algorithm~2 computes a partial update using a subroutine---\texttt{SampleClique} (Algorithm \ref{a:sample}) ---that generates only a sparse spanning tree among the neighbors. By sampling only a subset of the potential fill-ins (roughly $O(n)$ edges as opposed to $O(n^2)$ in the deterministic case, where $n = |\mathcal N_k|$), AC maintains sufficient connectivity while dramatically reducing the number of fill-ins. This selective approach lowers both the computational and memory costs associated with the Schur-complement update \cite{sachdeva2023simple}. In addition, letting $G$ be the lower triangular factor computed using AC, we have $\mathbb{E}(GG^\top) = L$, which was proven previously~\cite{gao2023robust, kyng2016approximate}.
% In contrast, AC2 is a more robust variant where each matrix entry is split into two “multi-entries” (or two samples are taken for each original entry). 
% \xslnote{Need to cite numerical accuracy in expectation.}

The reduced fill-in not only minimizes the memory and communication cost but also affects the dependency structure in the subsequent steps. Moreover, by reducing the arithmetic intensity (ratio of compute flops to memory operations) per vertex (expected to be $O(1)$), AC shifts the computational burden away from dense matrix operations and towards lightweight, probabilistic computations.

The expected run time of this algorithm is $O(M\log(N))$ ~\cite{kyng2016approximate}, where $M$ is the number of edges, and $N$ is the number of vertices. Experiments have demonstrated better numerical quality when sorting on \Cref{line:sort} of \Cref{a:sample} is used.



