To represent the elimination order obtained from symbolic factorization, we review the handy concept of an elimination tree (or e-tree), which is a data structure that captures the dependency relationships during the factorization of a sparse matrix. 

\begin{definition}
    Given an input (Laplacian) matrix $L \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$, its associated lower triangular Cholesky factor $G$, and a set of nodes $S = \{1, \dots, N\}$, the e-tree is a directed graph constructed by inserting an outgoing edge from each node $i \in S$ to $j$, where $j$ is the index of the first nonzero entry in $G_{:, i}$.
\end{definition}

In the context of Cholesky factorization, each node in the e-tree represents a column of the matrix, and the parent of a given node is typically defined as the column corresponding to the first nonzero entry below the diagonal in that column of the Cholesky factor. 
%The e-tree groups interconnected variables so that 
The nodes at different branches of the e-tree can be processed in parallel, thereby maximizing parallelism. 

% In classical Cholesky, an e-tree captures deterministic dependencies arising from complete clique updates; however, in the randomized variant (Algorithm \ref{a:rchol}), the sparser fill-in pattern creates a more relaxed dependency graph that can be exploited for parallelism.
