Fig. \ref{fig:mat_pattern} shows an example of an e-tree corresponding to a matrix. For any vertex $i$, once all vertices that have incoming edges into $i$ are eliminated, $i$ is ready to be factorized. One can construct a level set using breadth-first-search starting from the root (9 in this case), and factorize each level in parallel.

