In some sense, e-tree contains the ``minimum" dependency. For example, as shown by row 4 of the Cholesky factor in \cref{fig:mat_pattern}, vertex 4 receives Schur-complement updates from $\{0, 1, 2\}$. However, the elimination graph only shows an arrow from 2 to 4. This is because vertex 4's direct dependency on vertices 0 and 1 is already fused into the path $0 \rightarrow 1 \rightarrow 2 \rightarrow 4$. Fusing is exactly why the definition of the e-tree selects the first nonzero entry (the path will eventually add in the other nonzero entries). If the algorithm executes according to the dependency chain, then once vertex 2 finishes, vertices 0 and 1 also finish, so there is no need to explicitly store the connections $0 \rightarrow 4$ and $1 \rightarrow 4$. Hence, the e-tree is quite memory-efficient.

\Cref{fig:after_one_step} shows a possible configuration of the spanning tree that the neighbors of vertex 0 of the graph in \cref{fig:mat_pattern} can form after one step of the randomized Cholesky. This spanning tree enables $\{1, 2, 4\}$ to be factorized in parallel, which improves over e-tree's sequential requirement: $\{1 \rightarrow 2 \rightarrow 4\}$. Clearly, e-tree can be unnecessarily restrictive. 

To understand the source of parallelism, we first make the following observation about dependency structure in \cref{lemma:reachability} (originally stated and proved by Rose and Tarjan ~\cite{rosetarjan78}). 
\begin{lemma}
    In the classical Cholesky setting, given a graph $G = (V, E)$ and an factorization ordering (i.e., labeling the vertices with numbers). Define the dependencies of $i \in V$ to be vertices that must be eliminated before the algorithm can eliminate $i$. For any vertex $i$ in the graph, its dependency will include nodes that are reachable in the graph through a path that contains only vertices with label smaller than $i$. This means that $i$ will depend on $j$ iff $j < i$, and there exist path $\{i \rightarrow p_1 \rightarrow p_2 \ldots, p_t \rightarrow j\}$, such that $p_1, \ldots, p_t < i$. There is no ordering requirement among $p_i$'s. We will denote existing path between $i, j$ with the previous property (intermediate vertices on path smaller than $i$) $p^{*}(i, j)$. For each pair $(i, j)$, there may exist multiple $p^{*}(i,j)$.
    \label{lemma:reachability}
\end{lemma}

%\xslnote{This is the well-known "path theorem" already proved in~\cite{rosetarjan78} (Theorem 1). We can simply cite that here.}

% The proof for $\cref{lemma:reachability}$\david{i used label and ref for some sections etc, should I switch over to using cref instead?} is as follows. Suppose $i$ depends on $j$, then clearly $i$ must be reachable from $j$, and $i > j$. Hence, assume there exist path $p = {i  \rightarrow p_1, \ldots, p_n \rightarrow j}$ that connects $i$ from $j$. If there exists $k$ such that $p_k > i > j$, then $p_k$ ``severs" the connection since $p_k$ must be factored after both $i, j$, which means the impact of $j$ can't be propagated to $i$. Hence, if no path satisfy the condition, then $i$ won't depend on $j$ because all propagations get severed by the larger vertex in the path. Therefore, by contradiction, there must exist a path that satisfy $p_1, \ldots, p_n < i$. Going the other direction, if there exist a path $p = {i  \rightarrow p_1, \ldots, p_n \rightarrow j}$ satisfying the condition, then regardless how the element in the path is ordered, we see that eliminating some element $p_k$ would form connection between $p_{k - 1}$ and $p_{k + 1}$. Eventually the impact of $j$ will be propagated to $i$. Hence, $i$ depend on $j$.
  The intuition behind the lemma is that the modifications made by $j$ will eventually propagate to $i$, given that the labels of the vertices on the path are smaller than $i$. As an example, in \cref{fig:mat_pattern}, we see that $3$ does not depend on 2 because $p^{*}(3, 2)$ doesn't exist, as vertex 5 blocks the propagation because it's bigger than 3. On the other hand, vertex $5$ depend on $4$ because we can find $p^{*}(5, 4) = \{5 
\rightarrow 2 \rightarrow 0 \rightarrow 4\}$.

 From the previous observation, it's easy to see that sparsified sampling improves parallelism by decreasing reachability. If we run classical Cholesky and eliminate $0$, then a clique would form among the set $\{1, 2, 4, 8\}$. The vertices will be completely reachable from each other. For instance, one example of $p^{*}(4, 1)$ is the trivial direct connection $4 \rightarrow 1$. 
However, in the spanning tree example illustrated by $\cref{fig:after_one_step}$, vertex 8 in the random spanning tree ``severed" the connections between $\{1, 2, 4\}$. $p^{*}(4, 1)$ no longer exist, which enables vertices 1 and 4 to be eliminated in parallel. 

In summary, for any vertex pair $(i, j)$, the existence of $p^{*}(i, j) = \{i  \rightarrow p_1, \ldots, p_n \rightarrow j\}$ no longer implies dependency because the elimination of $p_k$ ($1 \leq k \leq n$) might not connect $p_{k - 1}$ and $p_{k + 1}$. 
In other words $p^{*}(i, j)$ becomes probabilistic rather than guaranteed. This provides additional opportunities for parallelism.

