\subsection{Descriptive Statistics}
Table~\ref{tab:explo} shows descriptive statistics of the cleaned \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} dataset. With the number of channels and groups being almost the same, our dataset can for example be used to investigate whether conspiracy theory related discussion arises organically through conversation in discussion groups, or whether agenda-setting by a small number of users in broadcast channels is more prevalent. We find that media files like \textit{images}, \textit{videos}, and \textit{voice messages} make up 26.4\% of all messages. While they account for 38.1\% of all forwarded messages, for original messages only 16.9\% are media files. This difference makes the analysis of the content of media files particularly relevant for investigating content dissemination. The distinction between forwarded messages and original messages also serves to estimate the scope of the conspiracy related German-language Telegram discourse, which we will leverage in Section ``Validation and Completeness'' below to gauge the completeness of the dataset. 

