A common aspect of social media platforms is high inequality, where few drivers (authors, chats) are responsible for most of the content/attention. In Fig.~\ref{fig:distributions} we show this to be true in the \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} as well. The probability distributions of messages and unique authors per chat (Fig.~\ref{fig:distributions}A) have very wide ranges, with the largest chats having on the order of $10^4$ authors and $10^6$ messages. At the same time, most chats have less than $10$ messages and authors. Due to the uncertainty around the dataset sampling scheme, we refrain from e.g. fitting power-law distributions as the sampling can heavily bias both measured exponents and model selection of the best-fitted distribution (power-law vs lognormal). Regardless, the distributions are clearly heavy-tailed.

Grouping the messages by unique authors and chats (Fig.~\ref{fig:distributions}B), we see that $80\%$ of all messages belong to the top $12.1\%$ and $3.1\%$ of chats and authors, respectively. This is reflected in the very high Gini indexes of $G=0.85$ (chats) and $G=0.94$ (authors). This high level of concentration is comparable with what is observed in other social media platforms~\cite{orellana-rodriguez_attention_2018, zhu_attention_2016}.














\subsection{Transcription}
At the time of this publication, transcription of all files with audio tracks is ongoing. New files will be iteratively added to the online dataset. For the transcription process, we used the \textit{openai/whisper-large-v3-turbo} model, which was the latest \textit{whisper} model available at the time of publication~\cite{radford2022whisper}. To boost the transcription speed, we combined the model with \textit{FlashAttention-2}~\cite{dao2023flashattention2} which resulted in a transcription rate of around 500x realtime. This increases the amount of text in our dataset from 1,490M words in the written messages to a total of approximately 2,103M words (estimation of video file words, see Table~\ref{tab:transcribe}) the text corpus by 41\%. As media files comprise a considerable share of all forwarded messages, transcribed messages are an important carrier of information between chats. A feature of the text generated via transcription is that it consists of a well-defined vocabulary and therefore has no typos or alternative spellings. While this precludes the analysis of textual characteristics related to these features, it might allow for more accurate text analysis and topic identification and requires less pre-processing before further analysis. 

