\section{Discussion and Conclusions}
In this paper, we present the \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} dataset, a large-scale collection of more than 6,000 Telegram chats collected via snowball-sampling, focusing primarily on German-language discussions in the realm of conspiracy theories, misinformation, and political extremism in the time from 2016 to 2022. Notably, posting activity in the dataset peaks during the COVID-19 pandemic and at key political events of the time, indicating heightened and potentially coordinated online activity in these special interest groups. 

With the present data publication, we provide a cleaned and validated version of the dataset that is easy to access and re-use for other researchers. We clarify questions around the ethics of using the dataset as well as the terms of use. The dataset contains digital trace data of individuals. While we pseudonymised usernames, the dataset is not completely anonymised and in principle allows for the re-identification of individuals based on publicly disclosed information contained in the data. We release this dataset because we believe the benefit of being able to study the spread of conspiracy theories in a completely unregulated online environment at scale during a time of crisis outweighs the risk of infringing on user expectations and privacy. However, we do not intend this dataset to be used for the profiling of individuals and strongly caution against any attempts of re-identification.

Rather than relying on data collection from the Telegram API, the dataset was scraped with an automated approach via Telegram desktop clients that enabled the download of multimedia content, e.g. images, videos and voice messages. While the raw dataset was accessible in the Internet Archive before, we make it easier to use for research by providing an assessment of its completeness and scope, parsing and cleaning it. Furthermore and next to the large corpus of text messages, we provide transcriptions of voice messages and videos, enriching the corpus with more than three million files which we make accessible for further analysis. The \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} is -- to our knowledge -- the only large-scale Telegram dataset that includes transcribed audio content. Based on our qualitative observations of the chats in the dataset, the discussion is often driven by video or voice message contributions while text messages are mostly used to comment on this multimedia content. Therefore, by ignoring multimedia content -- as is often-times the case in research that uses digital trace data -- large parts of the discussion are missed. With our work we therefore aim to enable research into the role of multimodal content in spreading conspiratorial narratives. 

In terms of information contained in the \textit{Schwurbelarchiv}, one substantial limitation of our dataset is the lack of unique user identifiers. As users can freely choose their usernames, the lack of access to user IDs makes it impossible to determine with certainty whether different authors are truly distinct individuals or whether posts with the same username originate from the same user. However, we believe the assumption that different usernames largely indicate activity by different individuals is warranted. To address this constraint, we plan to investigate text features and variance in temporal messaging patterns in the future to distinguish between authors who may share the same name.

We assessed the completeness of our dataset by comparing it to the content of comparable Telegram datasets~\cite{pushshift, mohrthesis, zehring}. This comparison also serves to estimate the scope of conspiracy-related discourse on Telegram during the COVID-19 pandemic in German speaking countries. On the chat level the overlap with other datasets is low, with a maximum of 13\% of the chats shared with~\citet{mohrthesis}. This low overlap can largely be explained by a difference in inclusion criteria used to select chats in the different dataset, and differences in the temporal scope of the data sets. Another way to gauge the completeness of our dataset regarding conspiracy-related discourse is to look at message forwards. Here, we found that 47.9\% of incoming forwarded messages are also contained as original messages in our dataset. On the flip side, this means that around half of the forwarded messages originate in chats that are not contained in our dataset. We therefore estimate that we cover around half of the conspiracy-related discourse happening in predominantly German-language Telegram chats at the time in our dataset. As we have no reason to believe that the selection of chats included in our dataset is systematically biased in any way, other than being centred on conspiracy-related discourse in German, we believe that the \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} is an adequate resource for the study of conspiracy theories in the given temporal and geographic scope. 

That being said, another limitation of our dataset relates to the unknown point in time at which data was collected for each chat. As has been reported by~\citet{buehling}, messages on Telegram are deleted relatively frequently, and deletion patterns are not random. We estimate large portions of our dataset, particularly towards the end of the observation period, to have been collected via streaming and therefore be relatively unaffected by message deletion. However, we cannot determine the point at which streaming started for each chat. Nevertheless, we note that the other datasets covering a similar geographical and temporal scope have all been collected by bulk download through the Telegram API~\cite{zehring, mohrthesis, pushshift}. Therefore, these data sets are subject to increasing message deletion the farther a point in time is away from the time of data collection as well. By including both bulk-downloaded and streamed portions of chat content, the \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} is likely more complete in terms of deleted messages than other data sets.

Next to the content of the messages, our data offers insights into the dynamics of information sharing and highlights how message frequency, group activity, and author engagement are distributed and evolve over time. The activity of authors in groups exhibits heavy-tailed distributions that approximate a power-law. This is consistent with activity patterns across social media platforms, such as Twitter~\cite{orellana-rodriguez_attention_2018}. Furthermore, activity distribution is very uneven, with 80\% of messages being posted in the top 12.1\% of chats 80\% of messages being posted by the top 3\% of authors.

The \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} data can also be analysed from a network perspective by treating groups or authors as nodes and linking them according to shared membership or participation. This approach reveals topological features such as common affiliations or frequently co-occurring authors. Future research could harness these networks to investigate community structures, track the spread of (mis-)information and narratives, and identify influential participants across different discussions.

In summary, the \textit{Schwurbelarchiv} provides broad utility for research in social network analysis, linguistic analysis, political science, and information studies, particularly in the realm of conspiracy theories. Future work can build on the dataset to investigate the dynamics of misinformation spread, conduct topic modelling, explore the influence of real-world events on user behaviour in online communities, and analyse the dynamics of conspiracy-related discourse in individual chats in a more qualitative way.

