\subsection{Adjustable Autonomy}

To facilitate trustworthy human-agent collaboration in LMA systems, adjustable autonomy, which enables humans and multi-agent systems to share control~\cite{10.5555/1838194.1838204,10.1007/11780519_23,10.1145/1647314.1647328,10.1177/0018720811420427}, can be leveraged to increase the reliability and trustworthiness of the system~\cite{10.1007/978-3-642-36981-0_57}.

Nonetheless, all is not lost, yet. Some of the characteristics of autonomous agents/systems that are observed to increase the users' trust within the system are anthropomorphic features like natural language interaction and human-like reasoning~\cite{WAYTZ2014113}. LLMs are observed to be proficient in interacting in natural language and answering users' questions in human-like manner while keeping track of the entire conversation~\cite{10062688}. A structured and systematic approach to interacting the LLMs such as implementing chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting has been observed to elicit reasoning capabilities as well~\cite{NEURIPS2022_9d560961}.

