\subsection{FDE boundary value problems}
\subsubsection{Basset--Boussinesq--Oseen equation}
As mentioned in the introduction, the matrix approximation to the FIO is also instrumental in solving FDEs. Our third example is the Basset--Boussinesq--Oseen equation 
\begin{align}
v'(t) +  \mD^{1/2}_t v(t) + v(t) = 0, \text{~~s.t.~~} v(0) = 1, \label{bbo}
\end{align}
where the fractional derivative operator is defined in the Caputo sense. This classical fractional differential equation models the motion of spherical particles in viscous fluids. Following the standard approach of integration reformulation, we let
\begin{align*}
v(t) = \mI^1 u(t) + 1,
\end{align*}
which transforms \cref{bbo} into an FIE:
\begin{align*}
u(t) + \mI^{1/2} u(t) + \mI^1 u(t) + 1 = 0.
\end{align*}
The solution is sought as a series expansion in $\{Q_n^{0,1/2}(x)\}_{n=0}^{\infty}$. \Cref{fig:sol} shows the computed solution, while \cref{fig:coeffs&error} illustrates the spectral decay of the JFP coefficients along with the Cauchy error, measured as the 2-norm difference between the coefficients obtained from the $N \times N$ and $(N+1) \times (N+1)$ systems.
